constraints': 'UNEXTRACTED', 'failure_mode': 'Occupational injuries', 'confidence': 'LOW'}, 'source': {'source_id': 'doi:10.1080/17457300.2026.2720847', 'source_name': 'semantic_scholar', 'content_hash': 'ce52edd4ef7c1b1b7324a92144c30ce28d4b7039579b004153f48cc3a5d0fd38', 'title': 'Advancing farm machinery safety: a review of injury risks, mitigation strategies, and technological innovations.', 'retrieval_timestamp': '2026-09-13T20:38:16.532191+00:00', 'source_uri': 'https://doi.org/10.1080/17457300.2026.2720847', 'doi': '10.1080/17457300.2026.2720847'}, 'provenance': {'custody': 'inherited_from_evidence_item (Art. VI — the existing custody chain; no competing ledger)', 'extraction_provider': 'localqwen', 'extraction_model': 'qwen3-4b', 'extraction_status': 'OK', 'prompt_hash': 'e490c493e9f03e6ca9e144299fb97966123d85b86fd9d25ba21b3548a57709a4', 'output_hash': 'f29f3010c3d16339b61d37993825b05a685690737e472039b89cd42966dfa05d', 'extraction_retry_note': None, 'extracted_at': '2026-09-13T20:46:14+00:00Z'}, 'record_text': "Advancing farm machinery safety: a review of injury risks, mitigation strategies, and technological innovations. Farm machinery significantly enhances agricultural productivity but remains a leading source of occupational injuries, particularly in resource-limited settings. This comprehensive review synthesises the primary human-, equipment-, environmental-, and weather-related causes of such injuries and critically evaluates mitigation strategies, incorporating new evidence on the interplay between machinery reliability and operator safety. A systematic review integrated literature published 2000-2025 across multiple databases. Intervention effectiveness was appraised using a multi-step methodology combining GRADE-based quality assessment, statistical inter-rater agreement (Cohen's κ  =  0.84), and internal reliability testing (Cronbach's α  =  0.83). The findings reveal that human factors, including inadequate training, fatigue, and cognitive decline, are the most prevalent risk contributors, followed by equipment-related issues such as outdated machinery and poor maintenance, and context-specific environmental and weather hazards. High-impact interventions include mandatory operator training, preventive and predictive maintenance protocols, retrofitting safety-critical components, and deploying sensor-based hazard detection. The newly integrated reliability framework highlights four mutually reinforcing pillars-design, maintenance, operations, and management systems-that demonstrate how engineering reliability directly reduces hazardous failures and improves safety metrics. This evidence-driven synthesis underscores the need to combine human factors interventions, ergonomic design, environmental adaptations, weather preparedness, and engineering reliability improvements to achieve the strongest safety outcomes, especially when supported by policy enforcement and training systems. "}, {'item_id': 'title:a8cb51aa213be35e', 'structured_hash': '4c13c4436b487a828846d1135d6419c8b7d2068ac967dfc06ed781a3d170f549', 'extraction_summary': {'n_fields_valid': 6, 'n_fields_unextracted': 3, 'n_fields_untrusted_demoted': 1, 'mechanism_extracted': True}, 'fields': {'claim': 'The need for systematic research on wind turbine design, operation, and maintenance due to increasing growth of wind turbine systems', 'observed_effect': 'Failure of some parts of wind turbine systems is frequent, although undesirable', 'system': 'Wind turbine systems', 'intervention': 'UNEXTRACTED', 'mechanism': 'The thesis focuses on the condition monitoring of wind turbine gearboxes, taking into account the varying loads they endure', 'boundary_conditions': 'Operating under challenging environmental conditions', 'constraints': 'UNEXTRACTED', 'failure_mode': 'UNEXTRACTED', 'confidence': 'LOW'}, 'source': {'source_id': 'title:a8cb51aa213be35e', 'source_name': 'core', 'content_hash': '7358de2b76f256ac30df2955d70749b35ff9da3273a45d41300dbbe60a6c6a88', 'title': 'Accounting for nonstationarity in the condition monitoring of wind turbine gearboxes', 'retrieval_timestamp': '2026-09-13T20:38:16.532243+00:00', 'source_uri': '', 'doi': None}, 'provenance': {'custody': 'inherited_from_evidence_item (Art. VI — the existing custody chain; no competing ledger)', 'extraction_provider': 'localqwen', 'extraction_model': 'qwen3-4b', 'extraction_status': 'OK', 'prompt_hash': '07d9355c5797cd9c181079bf1591c5cb290eb8d020867194d4c3edce6f108e8c', 'output_hash': '57d8b95d3290346a8848cb39309ce7ff165418e3e6ae4fe2127a08dedc2dd76b', 'extraction_retry_note': None, 'extracted_at': '2026-09-13T20:47:13+00:00Z'}, 'record_text': 'Accounting for nonstationarity in the condition monitoring of wind turbine gearboxes Increasing growth of wind turbine systems suggests a more systematic research around their design, operation and maintenance is needed. These systems operate under challenging enviromental conditions and failure of some of their parts, for the time being, is frequent, although undesirable. Wind turbine gearboxes, more particularly, seem to be so problematic that some wind turbine designs avoid including them. Structural health monitoring and condition monitoring of wind turbines appear to be necessary in order to determine the condition and lifespan of the wind turbine components and the drivetrain respectively. In this way reparative actions could be taken whenever needed resulting in reduction of maintenance costs. 



This thesis focuses on the condition monitoring of wind turbine gearboxes, taking into account the varying loads that they endure. Currently, the vibration-based damage detection methods used in real life wind turbine condition monitoring systems are based on conventional methods that generally fail to detect damage at its early stage under the operational conditions observed in wind turbines. Load and speed variations of the drivetrain that are observed commonly in wind turbines influence the vibration signals and can possibly affect potential damage features. This shows a demand for effective methods for early damage detection. Developments in the area of advanced signal processing should be examined and applied in damage detection of wind turbine gearboxes. Methods from time-frequency analysis, time-scale analysis, pattern recognition, multivariate statistics and econometrics are examined in this study in a condition monitoring context. 



